<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bosphorus</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bosphorus is a narrow strait in northwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> connecting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sea-of-marmara">Sea of Marmara</a> and separating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> stood on its European shore, controlling traffic between the two seas. This strategic position underlay the foundation and long survival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a>'s capital.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bosphorus.jpg" alt="Bosphorus" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Interiot · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turkish_Strait_disambig.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
